    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey prof, i'm really struggling with the intuition behind the fourier transform. i get the formulas, but i don't really \"get\" what's actually happening when a signal gets decomposed into frequencies. can you help me understand the physical intuition of it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can think of a signal as a complex mixture of many pure sine waves, where the Fourier Transform acts like a searchlight revealing which frequencies are present and how strong they are. Just as white light is made of all the visible colors, a messy signal is constructed by adding up simple harmonic motions at different rates. The transformation essentially matches your specific signal against these simple waveforms to calculate their \"contribution\" or amplitude in a frequency domain, making the hidden structure of the noise visible.",
