    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, but what i mean is, how does the math actually \"pick out\" a specific frequency? like, if i have a messy signal with a lot of random noise, how does the transform know to focus on one specific wave instead of just getting overwhelmed by the rest?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The math doesn't actually \"pick out\" the signal; instead, it calculates how much every possible sine wave overlaps with your data using dot products. When a sine wave matches your signal's rhythm perfectly, its overlap creates a large value, while random noise tends to cancel itself out or average to near zero. You can visualize this as tuning a radio knob: only at the exact right frequency do the waves line up in phase to produce a strong signal, whereas other frequencies drift out of sync and disappear in the math.",
      "source": "target",
